Maybe you have a different understanding of what I mean here by gambling.
Walking around in the dungeons I certainly have not meant to be "gambling",
here.[*]

> [...]
> 
> Not doing something that has a 67% failure rate because "I don't gamble" is
> silly.

Nonsense. It's not silly. If anything I'd call it intelligent resource
management; don't waste a resource with a 67% probability if you don't
need to. - Remember, there was no need to have those scrolls. - Only in
cases where you have to do some action, say, to not die, you can take
the probabilities of success of the available actions into account to
select the most promising one, and if there's just one option you have
to swallow the bitter pill and gamble. And if you have (as so typical
in Nethack) a lot of options you choose the ones that fit best, either
in tactical decisions or also considering strategic goals. In the given
case I don't needed to waste valuable marker charges for a 33% success
chance of something not needed in the given context. Specifically items
with high generation probabilities (like the scroll of enchant weapon)
you will usually just find (typically sooner than later) lying around.
No need to gamble.

Janis

[*] In some way you can also see walking around also as gambling; but
for a reasonable practical approach it's not really helpful comparing
the failure chance of writing the unknown scroll successfully (67%)
with getting poisoned by a pit when walking around (say, 1%). You can
avoid the traps (but also only to a certain degree) e.g. by searching,
but then the risk that you starve rather soon increases significantly.
It's that sort of risk decisions that I not long ago explained in an
other context; if you can reduce your chance to stumble into pits, say
from 1% to 0.2% by simultaneously increasing the risk of starving from
5% to 50%, then you shouldn't do that, because the former event has a
lager probability to not happen. They call that risk management.



